# bulk.tkr — the BULK channel: the whole host-configuration corpus, ONE build.
#
# THE CHANNEL RULE (owner 2026-07-26): a variation in SOURCE needs a separate FILE inside one
# project; only a variation in BUILD CONFIGURATION earns a separate build. Every scenario in this
# file builds for the host with no env of its own beyond the selector, so every scenario in this file
# runs off ONE build — the runner compiles a project's `.tkp` at most once per `.tkr` (`RegrBuilt`),
# and `TEKO_REGR_ENTRY` (read by `main.tks`) picks which member namespace's `entry()` runs.
#
# IT REPLACES FIVE PROJECTS THAT COST FIVE BUILDS: di_same_name_cross_ns,
# iface_value_name_collision_factory, member_const_cross_ns_pub_inherit, qualified_optional and
# same_bare_method_dispatch. Their Features are below VERBATIM, in that order; the only edit is that
# each project's OWN scenario — the one that used to run with no selector set — now names the member
# namespace its body moved into (`p_<project>`), because in a merged build there is no such thing as
# "the project's own body".
#
# TWO SCENARIOS WERE DELIBERATELY RENAMED, and no other:
#
#   * `left::Svc and right::Svc, sharing the bare name Svc, each inject their own namespace's Face`
#     -> `di_left::Svc_di and di_right::Svc_di, sharing the bare name Svc_di, …`
#   * `left::Svc and right::Svc, sharing the bare name Svc, each dispatch tag_of to their own instance`
#     -> `sbmd_left::Svc and sbmd_right::Svc, …`
#
# Both projects declared a `left` and a `right` namespace and a directory IS a namespace, so the four
# could not keep two names between them (see bulk.tkp). The names now say which namespaces and which
# bare declaration each scenario actually reads; what each asserts is untouched.
#
# THE DECONFLICTION RULE, and the ONE thing it may never do. Merging five projects puts every
# top-level name they declared into one build, and the language does NOT yet resolve every position
# canonically (structural-trait derivation, a trait constraint, a top-level `fn` passed as a value and
# a top-level `const` all still key off the BARE last segment — the #284/#290 defect class, fixed for
# DI and open elsewhere). So a bare name declared by namespaces that came from DIFFERENT projects is
# suffixed with its project's tag, and a bare name whose owners all came from the SAME project is left
# exactly as it was: the merge may not CREATE a collision, and it may not REMOVE one either. The
# collisions each folded project pinned survive verbatim — `aa::Thing`/`bb::Thing`,
# `codec::Reader`/`stream::Reader`, `sbmd_left::Svc`/`sbmd_right::Svc`/`aaa_decoy::Svc`,
# `di_left::Face`/`di_right::Face`, the root `Base` beside `sub::Sub` — and so does the intra-project
# `Box`/`Box<T>` pair whose monomorph stop this channel still reports.
#
# THE ONE SCENARIO HERE THAT DOES NOT SHARE THE BUILD is the codegen-stage KNOWN-STOP pin, which
# names its own `Given source` case file: a build that reaches codegen is one whose checker PASSED,
# so a stop at that stage cannot share a failing build with anything. It costs one build, and it is
# the only one this channel pays beyond its own.

Feature: di_same_name_cross_ns — same-bare-name DI providers stay keyed by canonical namespace

  Scenario: di_left::Svc_di and di_right::Svc_di, sharing the bare name Svc_di, each inject their own namespace's Face
    Given env = ["TEKO_REGR_ENTRY=p_di_same_name_cross_ns"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 42

